      "QUICK ANSWER \u2014 What is due diligence in the OHADA zone?\n\nDue diligence is a pre-transactional audit covering the legal, financial, tax, regulatory, and organizational aspects of a target company. In the OHADA zone, it must include verification of compliance with the AUSCGIE (company law), the AUDCIF (accounting), BCEAO (UEMOA) or COBAC (CEMAC) requirements for financial institutions, and AML/CFT regulations. It validates the acquisition price, negotiates guarantee clauses (representations & warranties), and identifies post-closing risks.",

      "MAIN CONTENT\n\n1. The OHADA legal framework applicable to acquisitions\n\n1.1 The revised AUSCGIE 2014: rules for mergers and acquisitions\n\nThe OHADA Uniform Act on Commercial Companies and Economic Interest Groups (AUSCGIE), revised in 2014, governs the rules applicable to share transfers, contributions in kind, and mergers in the 17 member states.\n\nMandatory due diligence verification points:\n- Articles of association regularity: compliance with AUSCGIE, share transfer clauses, preemption rights, shareholders' agreements\n- Shareholder traceability: up-to-date shareholder register, verifiable history of share transfers\n- Regulated agreements: identification and validation of contracts between the company and its directors or shareholders (articles 520 et seq. of the AUSCGIE)\n- Business asset transfers: compliance with publicity formalities (articles 185 et seq. of the AUSCGIE)\n- Capital and reserves: verification of full payment of share capital, compliance of dividend distributions\n\nSource: OHADA, revised AUSCGIE 2014 \u2014 ohada.com\n\n1.2 The revised AUDCIF 2017: accounting and financial statements\n\nThe OHADA Uniform Act on the Organization and Harmonization of Business Accounting (AUDCIF) sets the accounting framework for all companies in the zone. In due diligence, it is necessary to verify:\n- Production of mandatory financial statements: balance sheet, income statement, Cash Flow Statement (CFS), notes to the accounts\n- Compliance of accounting methods (provisions, depreciation, inventory valuation)\n- Absence of significant accounting restatements\n\nSource: OHADA, revised AUDCIF 2017 \u2014 ohada.com",

      "2. The 5 pillars of the KHEPRA DD\u2122 due diligence\n\nPillar 1 \u2014 Legal and Corporate Due Diligence\n\nObjective: validate legal ownership of shares and absence of major litigation.\n\nElements verified:\n- Articles of association and AGM minutes (AUSCGIE compliance)\n- Shareholder register and transfer history\n- Commercial leases and land titles (verification with the land registry)\n- Intellectual property (trademarks, patents, licenses)\n- Ongoing litigation and significant contractual obligations\n- Sectoral licenses and approvals (BCEAO, COBAC, ministries)\n- Regulated agreements and conflicts of interest\n\nCritical red flag: unregistered or disputed land titles. According to the World Bank, the land registry quality index in Sub-Saharan Africa is among the lowest in the world, making verification of property rights essential.\n\nSource: World Bank, Doing Business Report 2020 \u2014 worldbank.org\n\nPillar 2 \u2014 Financial and Accounting Due Diligence\n\nObjective: validate the quality of financial statements and identify latent losses.\n\nElements verified:\n- Review of balance sheet, income statement, and CFS for 3 fiscal years\n- Analysis of provisions: doubtful receivables, obsolete inventory, litigation\n- Verification of bank reconciliation and outstanding credit exposures\n- Identification of non-recurring charges and income\n- Calculation of real WCR and post-acquisition projection\n- Analysis of loan portfolio quality (NPL rate)\n\nCritical red flag: under-provisioning of doubtful receivables. An NPL coverage ratio below 70% means that the reported result is fiction.\n\nPillar 3 \u2014 Tax Due Diligence\n\nObjective: identify tax reassessment risks and validate the tax position.\n\nElements verified:\n- Tax returns for the last 5 years\n- Tax reassessment risks (VAT, corporate tax, customs duties)\n- Loss carryforwards and tax claims\n- Applicable tax treaties (especially for foreign investors)\n\nPillar 4 \u2014 Regulatory and Compliance Due Diligence\n\nObjective: validate compliance with sectoral regulators.\n\nElements verified:\n- BCEAO (UEMOA) or COBAC (CEMAC) compliance for SFDs/EMFs\n- AML/CFT compliance (UEMOA Directive No. 02/2015 / CEMAC Regulation No. 01/16/CEMAC/UMAC/CM of 11 April 2016, abrogating Regulation No. 01/03 of 2003)\n- Valid sectoral approvals\n- OHADA compliance (AUDCIF, AUSCGIE)\n\nCritical red flag: absence of a valid approval or license. Without BCEAO or COBAC approval, a financial institution cannot legally collect deposits.\n\nSource: BCEAO, Instruction No. 010-08-2010 \u2014 bceao.int; COBAC, EMF-2017 Regulation \u2014 beac.int (COBAC is a prudential supervisory body established within the BEAC by the Convention of 16 October 1990 revised on 6 April 2001, endowed with functional autonomy)\n\nPillar 5 \u2014 HR and Organizational Due Diligence\n\nObjective: assess risks related to human resources and management structure.\n\nElements verified:\n- Workforce mapping and employment contracts\n- Ongoing labor disputes\n- Compensation structure and social benefits\n- Evaluation of key competencies\n- Organizational diagnosis (organizational chart, processes, IT systems)\n\nCritical red flag: excessive dependence on the founder. The absence of a documented management succession plan creates a major post-acquisition risk.",

      "3. Business valuation methods in the OHADA zone\n\n3.1 Asset-based approach\n\nValue = Revalued assets \u2014 Real liabilities\n\nRelevant for companies with a significant real estate or industrial asset base. Limitation: undervalues service companies and intangible assets (brand, customer base, know-how).\n\n3.2 Multiples approach\n\nValuation by comparison with similar transactions. In Africa, comparable transaction data is scarce. AVCA (African Private Equity and Venture Capital Association) publishes annual reports on private capital activity in Africa.\n\nSource: AVCA, African Private Capital Activity Report \u2014 avca-africa.org\n\n3.3 Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) approach\n\nThe most rigorous method for growing companies. Requires:\n- Free Cash Flow projections over 5 years\n- Discount rate adjusted for country risk\n- Terminal value calculated by multiple or perpetual growth\n\nFormula: Value = Sum of FCFt / (1 + WACC)^t + Terminal value / (1 + WACC)^n\n\nReference rates:\n- Risk-free rate: BCEAO Treasury bonds (UEMOA) or BEAC bonds (CEMAC)\n- Country risk premium: 5% to 12% depending on country and sector\n\nSource: IMF, Global Financial Stability Report \u2014 imf.org",

      "OPERATIONAL CHECKLIST \u2014 KHEPRA DD\u2122 Checklist \u2014 Pre-Acquisition Due Diligence\n\nPhase A \u2014 Preparation (J-30 to J0)\n- Signature of confidentiality agreement (NDA)\n- Opening of virtual data room\n- Definition of DD scope (legal, financial, tax, regulatory, HR)\n- Formation of the DD team (lawyer, accountant, sector consultant)\n- Verification of 10 key documents: articles of association, minutes, shareholder register, last 3 balance sheets, CFS, tax returns, leases, land titles, approvals, organizational chart\n\nPhase B \u2014 Execution (J0 to J45)\n- Legal DD: AUSCGIE compliance, land titles, litigation, regulated agreements\n- Financial DD: accounting restatement, provisions, WCR, NPL, bank reconciliation\n- Tax DD: last 5 years, reassessment risks, tax treaties\n- Regulatory DD: BCEAO/COBAC, AML/CFT, approvals, OHADA\n- HR DD: workforce, contracts, disputes, organizational chart, IT\n\nPhase C \u2014 Synthesis and Reporting (J45 to J60)\n- Drafting of DD report with red flags and risks classified by criticality\n- Proposal for price adjustment or guarantee clauses\n- Structuring of SPA (Share Purchase Agreement)\n- Post-acquisition integration plan (100 days)\n\nObjective: deliverable in 60 days, red flags identified before the term sheet.",

      "CRITICAL RISKS \u2014 The 7 errors that destroy an acquisition in Africa\n\n1. Unregistered land titles: In countries where the land registry is incomplete, the buyer may discover post-closing that the target's factory land legally belongs to a third party. Potential cost: total loss of the asset.\n\n2. Uncollectible customer receivables: The average length of judicial proceedings in the OHADA zone is 2 to 5 years. A 500M FCFA unprovisioned receivable represents a real loss of 500M FCFA.\n\n3. Hidden debts: Debts to informal suppliers, undeclared tax debts, unrecorded social commitments. Impact: post-closing price adjustment of 10% to 30%.\n\n4. Founder dependence: The company relies on a single individual for client relationships, banking agreements, and regulatory approvals. Risk: business collapse if the founder leaves.\n\n5. Regulatory change: Changes to BCEAO/COBAC frameworks that could make the target's business model non-compliant. Example: increase in minimum capital requirements for SFDs.\n\n6. Under-provisioning: Not provisioning doubtful receivables improves the year's result but masks latent losses. The NPL coverage ratio must exceed 70%.\n\n7. AML/CFT non-compliance: A target not compliant with anti-money laundering requirements (UEMOA Directive No. 02/2015 / CEMAC Regulation No. 01/16/CEMAC/UMAC/CM of 11 April 2016, abrogating Regulation No. 01/03 of 2003) is ineligible for international financing and exposes the buyer to criminal sanctions.\n\nSource: OHADA; BCEAO; COBAC; World Bank, Doing Business Report",

      "PROPRIETARY METHODOLOGY \u2014 KHEPRA DD\u2122\n\nKHEPRA DD\u2122 is Khepra Experts' proprietary methodology for due diligence in the OHADA zone. It is based on 4 structured phases:\n\nPhase 1 \u2014 Discovery (J0 to J15): map the target and identify high-risk zones. Deliverables: indexed data room, list of missing documents, preliminary identification of red flags.\n\nPhase 2 \u2014 Deep Dive (J15 to J45): in-depth analysis of each DD pillar. Deliverables: legal, financial, tax, regulatory, and HR reports with accounting restatements.\n\nPhase 3 \u2014 Synthesis (J45 to J60): produce the synthesis report with recommendations. Deliverables: executive DD report (20 pages), list of risks classified by criticality (red/orange/green), proposal for price adjustment or SPA clauses.\n\nPhase 4 \u2014 Post-Closing (J60 to J160): support post-acquisition integration. Deliverables: 100-day integration plan, post-acquisition governance, turnaround plan if necessary.\n\nTotal duration: 60 days for DD, 100 additional days for integration.\nStandards: compliant with international due diligence practices, adapted to the OHADA context, verifiable sources only.",

      "CASE STUDY \u2014 Due diligence of a target SFD in the UEMOA zone\n\nContext: A private equity fund evaluates the acquisition of a category 2 SFD in Senegal, with 2.5B FCFA in assets and 180 employees.\n\nKHEPRA DD\u2122 findings:\n1. Red flag: The headquarters land title was not registered in the land registry. The founder held a customary title not transcribed. Risk: loss of headquarters in case of dispute.\n2. Red flag: The NPL coverage ratio was 42% (BCEAO threshold: 70%). Unprovisioned latent losses: 380M FCFA. Impact on valuation: -15% adjustment.\n3. Orange flag: The BCEAO approval was valid but the SFD category had not been updated following a capital increase. Risk: compliance injunction.\n4. Orange flag: Absence of management succession plan. The founder-CEO held all banking and regulatory relationships.\n\nActions taken:\n- Structuring a -12% price adjustment clause in the SPA\n- Establishment of a 200M FCFA escrow account to cover identified tax risks\n- Support in regularizing the land title before closing\n- Recruitment of a deputy CEO 6 months before transition\n\nResult: Acquisition completed at a price adjusted by -18% from the initial offer, with structured guarantees. The SFD achieved 22% growth in Year 1 after acquisition.",

      "SEO/IA FAQ\n\nQ: How long does due diligence take in the OHADA zone?\nR: A complete due diligence in the OHADA zone takes between 45 and 60 days. It covers 5 pillars: legal, financial, tax, regulatory, and HR. A Flash DD (identification of main red flags) can be completed in 5 days.\n\nQ: What are the mandatory documents for due diligence in the OHADA zone?\nR: The 10 key documents are: AUSCGIE-compliant articles of association, AGM minutes, shareholder register, last 3 balance sheets, CFS, tax returns (5 years), leases, land titles, sectoral approvals, organizational chart.\n\nQ: What is the difference between UEMOA and CEMAC due diligence?\nR: In the UEMOA zone, supervision is carried out by the BCEAO and the UMOA Banking Commission. In the CEMAC zone, it is COBAC. Prudential ratios differ: minimum solvency of 9.5% in UEMOA vs. 8% in CEMAC. Risk concentration limits are 75% in UEMOA vs. 45% in CEMAC.\n\nQ: How do you value a company in the OHADA zone?\nR: Three methods are used: asset-based (revalued assets \u2014 real liabilities), comparable multiples (AVCA), and DCF (discounted cash flows). DCF is recommended for growing companies. The risk-free reference rate is the BCEAO Treasury bond rate (UEMOA) or BEAC bond rate (CEMAC).\n\nCTA CONVERSION: Secure your next acquisition in Francophone Africa with the KHEPRA DD\u2122 methodology. Request a consultation for your due diligence."
