      "QUICK ANSWER \u2014 What is IMF/EMF licensing and why is it mandatory?\n\nLicensing is the prior authorization issued by the supervisory authority (BCEAO in UEMOA, COBAC in CEMAC) that authorizes an institution to collect public deposits. Without a license, deposit collection constitutes a criminal offense. Licensing validates the institution's credibility with regulators, depositors, and financial partners.",

      "MAIN CONTENT\n\n1. UEMOA regulatory framework \u2014 BCEAO and Instruction No. 010-08-2010\n\nThe BCEAO governs Decentralized Financial Systems (SFD) via Instruction No. 010-08-2010 on the conditions for licensing, organization, operation, and prudential supervision of SFDs in the UEMOA zone.\n\nSFD classification:\n- Category 1: deposit collection from members and lending\n- Category 2: public deposit collection and lending to third parties\n- Category 3: lending without deposit collection\n\nOnly Category 1 and 2 SFDs are subject to licensing.\n\nLicensing criteria:\n- Minimum paid-up share capital (set by the BCEAO and varying by category)\n- Quality of founders and directors (banking or microfinance experience, clean criminal record)\n- 5-year business plan demonstrating economic viability\n- Compliance with prudential requirements (ALM, AML/CFT, internal control)\n- Adequacy of IT systems and internal control\n\nSource: BCEAO, Instruction No. 010-08-2010 \u2014 bceao.int\n\n2. CEMAC regulatory framework \u2014 COBAC and EMF-2017 Regulation\n\nCOBAC governs Microfinance Institutions (EMF) via the EMF-2017 Regulation. EMFs are classified into the same three categories.\n\nKey difference: In CEMAC, the license is issued by the Minister of Finance of the country on the binding opinion of COBAC. In UEMOA, it is issued directly by the BCEAO.\n\nSource: COBAC, EMF-2017 Regulation \u2014 beac.int (COBAC is a prudential supervisory body established within the BEAC by the Convention of 16 October 1990 revised on 6 April 2001, endowed with functional autonomy)",

      "3. The steps of the licensing procedure \u2014 KHEPRA LICENSE\u2122 methodology\n\nPhase 1 \u2014 File preparation (J0 to J90)\n- Company incorporation in compliance with OHADA AUSCGIE\n- Development of a 5-year business plan with complete financial model\n- Drafting of internal policies (credit, ALM, AML/CFT, compliance)\n- Recruitment of directors and initial training\n- Implementation of information systems and internal control\n\nPhase 2 \u2014 Submission and review (J90 to J180)\n- Submission of complete file to the BCEAO\n- Response to requests for clarification\n- Preliminary site visit by supervisory services\n- Opinion of the UMOA Banking Commission\n\nPhase 3 \u2014 Issuance and launch (J180 to J270)\n- License issuance by the BCEAO\n- Publication in the Official Gazette\n- Effective opening to the public\n- First regulatory reporting\n\n4. The 10 documents required in a licensing file\n\n1. Licensing application signed by the founders\n2. Articles of association compliant with OHADA AUSCGIE\n3. List and CV of founders and directors (with criminal records)\n4. 5-year business plan (complete financial model)\n5. Internal policies: credit, ALM, AML/CFT, compliance, recovery\n6. Organizational chart and job descriptions\n7. Lease contract or land title for the premises\n8. Professional liability insurance certificate\n9. IT system technical file\n10. Minutes of the constituent assembly\n\nSource: BCEAO, Instruction No. 010-08-2010, Annex \u2014 bceao.int; COBAC, EMF-2017 Regulation, Annex \u2014 beac.int (COBAC is a prudential supervisory body established within the BEAC by the Convention of 16 October 1990 revised on 6 April 2001, endowed with functional autonomy)",

