      "SEO/IA FAQ\n\nQ: How long does the IMF/EMF licensing procedure take in the UEMOA zone?\nR: The licensing procedure takes between 6 and 9 months in the UEMOA zone (BCEAO). It comprises 3 phases: file preparation (J0-J90), BCEAO review (J90-J180), issuance and launch (J180-J270). An incomplete file can extend the procedure by 6 to 12 additional months.\n\nQ: What is the minimum capital to create an IMF in the UEMOA zone?\nR: The minimum capital is set by the BCEAO via Instruction No. 005-06-2010 and varies by SFD category. It is recommended to consult the BCEAO directly for current amounts, as they may be revised.\n\nQ: What is the difference between a UEMOA SFD and a CEMAC EMF?\nR: In the UEMOA zone, microfinance institutions are called SFD (Decentralized Financial Systems) and are governed by the BCEAO via Instruction No. 005-06-2010 (licensing). In the CEMAC zone, they are called EMF (Microfinance Institutions) and are governed by COBAC via the EMF-2017 Regulation. The licensing procedure differs: in UEMOA, the license is issued by the BCEAO; in CEMAC, it is issued by the Minister of Finance on the binding opinion of COBAC.\n\nCTA CONVERSION: Obtain your IMF/EMF license in 6 months with the KHEPRA LICENSE\u2122 methodology. Request a consultation."
