      "POSITIONING: Khepra Experts is a specialized advisory firm focused on regulatory compliance, due diligence, and ESG advisory in Francophone Africa. We support banks, SFDs/EMFs, and project promoters in structuring their ESG compliance and preparing for audits by international donors.",

      "QUICK ANSWER — What is ESG and why is it strategic?\n\nESG (Environment, Social, Governance) refers to a set of extra-financial evaluation criteria applied by institutional investors, multilateral development finance institutions and, progressively, prudential supervisors. Unlike traditional regulatory compliance — which rests on codified legal obligations — ESG primarily involves market expectations, financing agreements and voluntary commitments by institutions.\n\nIn French-speaking Africa, the IFC, BOAD, AfDB, AFD and European development banks require sustainability reports compliant with GRI, IFC or ISSB standards in their financing agreements. The BCEAO and COBAC are progressively strengthening the integration of climate and environmental risks into their supervisory approaches, without however having a fully codified ESG prudential framework comparable to European standards.",

      "MAIN CONTENT\n\n1. International ESG framework: reference standards\n\n1.1 The IFC Performance Standards\n\nThe International Finance Corporation (IFC), a member of the World Bank Group, adopted the Performance Standards on Environmental and Social Sustainability (2012, revised), applicable to financed projects. They must be distinguished from the IFC Sustainability Framework and the EHS Guidelines (Environmental, Health and Safety Guidelines).\n\nThe 8 official Performance Standards:\n1. Assessment and Management of Environmental and Social Risks and Impacts\n2. Labor and Working Conditions\n3. Resource Efficiency and Pollution Prevention and Management\n4. Community Health, Safety, and Security\n5. Land Acquisition and Involuntary Resettlement\n6. Biodiversity Conservation and Sustainable Management of Living Natural Resources\n7. Indigenous Peoples\n8. Cultural Heritage\n\nIn addition to the Performance Standards, the IFC requires the establishment of an Environmental and Social Management System (ESMS) to identify, assess and manage the environmental and social risks of a project.\n\nSource: IFC, Performance Standards on Environmental and Social Sustainability, 2012 — ifc.org\nSource: IFC, Sustainability Framework — ifc.org\nSource: IFC, Environmental, Health and Safety (EHS) Guidelines — ifc.org\n\n1.2 The Global Reporting Initiative (GRI)\n\nThe GRI Standards constitute the international framework for non-financial reporting. The current structure includes:\n- GRI 1: Foundation 2021\n- GRI 2: General Disclosures 2021\n- GRI 3: Material Topics 2021\n- Sector Standards (by industry)\n- GRI 200: Economic performance (GRI 201-207)\n- GRI 300: Environmental performance (GRI 301-308)\n- GRI 400: Social performance (GRI 401-419)\n\nSource: GRI, Universal Standards 2021 — globalreporting.org\n\n1.3 The UN Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI)\n\nLaunched in 2006, the PRI apply to institutional investors (asset managers, pension funds, insurance companies). They cover 6 principles for integrating ESG criteria into analysis, ownership, engagement and reporting. Adherence is voluntary and subject to annual reporting obligations.\n\nSource: PRI, Principles for Responsible Investment — unpri.org\n\n1.4 The ISSB and IFRS S1 / S2 standards\n\nThe International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB), created by the IFRS Foundation in 2021, has issued two foundational standards:\n- IFRS S1: Sustainability-related Financial Disclosures (June 2023) — requires disclosure of information about sustainability-related risks and opportunities material to the entity's prospects.\n- IFRS S2: Climate-related Disclosures (June 2023) — specifies climate-related disclosure requirements structured around the four TCFD pillars (governance, strategy, risk management, metrics and targets).\n\nThese standards apply for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2024, with progressive adoption by jurisdiction.\n\nSource: IFRS Foundation, ISSB Standards — ifrs.org\n\n1.5 The TCFD — Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures\n\nEstablished by the Financial Stability Board (FSB) in 2017, the TCFD proposes a disclosure framework structured around four pillars:\n1. Governance: Board and management roles and responsibilities regarding climate risks\n2. Strategy: impact of climate risks on strategy, financial outlook and planning\n3. Risk Management: processes for identifying, assessing and managing climate risks\n4. Metrics and Targets: indicators used to assess risks and opportunities\n\nThe TCFD was dissolved in October 2023, its functions transferred to the ISSB. Its recommendations remain operational and integrated into IFRS S2.\n\nSource: FSB, TCFD Recommendations — fsb-tcfd.org\n\n1.6 The SASB — Sustainability Accounting Standards Board\n\nIntegrated into the IFRS Foundation in 2022, the SASB develops sector-specific accounting standards (77 industries) to identify financially material ESG indicators. SASB focuses on financial materiality (impact of ESG on enterprise value), while GRI adopts a double materiality approach.\n\nSource: IFRS Foundation, SASB Standards — ifrs.org\n\n1.7 The TNFD — Taskforce on Nature-related Financial Disclosures\n\nLaunched in 2021, the TNFD transposes the TCFD logic to nature-related risks (biodiversity, ecosystems, ecosystem services). Its disclosure framework follows the TCFD four pillars (LEAP: Locate, Evaluate, Assess, Prepare).\n\nSource: TNFD, Nature-related Financial Disclosures Framework — tnfd.global\n\n1.8 The Equator Principles (EP4)\n\nAdopted in 2003 and revised in June 2020 (EP4), the Equator Principles apply to financial institutions financing infrastructure and industrial projects. They condition financing on environmental and social assessment compliant with IFC Performance Standards. 140 financial institutions in more than 40 countries have adopted them.\n\nSource: Equator Principles Association — equator-principles.com\n\n1.9 ISO standards applicable to ESG\n\n- ISO 14001: Environmental Management Systems — structured framework for environmental management, impact assessment and regulatory compliance.\n- ISO 26000: Social Responsibility — guidelines (non-certifiable) covering social responsibility, organizational ethics and stakeholder relations.\n- ISO 27001: Information Security Management Systems — data governance and cybersecurity framework applicable to ESG data governance.\n\nSource: ISO — iso.org\n\n1.10 Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) and Net Zero frameworks\n\nThe SBTi validates GHG emission reduction targets aligned with IPCC scenarios (1.5°C and well-below 2°C). Net Zero frameworks (Net-Zero Asset Owner Alliance, Race to Zero) govern sectoral decarbonization trajectories and commitments toward 2050.\n\nSource: SBTi — sciencebasedtargets.org",

      "2. Essential conceptual distinctions\n\n2.1 ESG vs CSR\n\nESG (Environment, Social, Governance) refers to an extra-financial evaluation framework used by institutional investors and financial markets, structured around quantified metrics, standardized reporting and integration into investment decisions.\n\nCSR (Corporate Social Responsibility) refers to a voluntary approach to ethical, social and environmental commitment that may include philanthropic actions and community partnerships without necessarily falling under an institutional reporting framework.\n\nESG is not CSR. ESG is a governance and reporting framework. CSR is a societal engagement approach.\n\n2.2 ESG vs regulatory compliance\n\nRegulatory compliance rests on laws, directives and binding instructions (AML/CFT, BCEAO/COBAC prudential ratios, SYSCOHADA). ESG primarily rests on voluntary standards (GRI, PRI, SBTi) and financing agreements.\n\nHowever, the boundary between the two is narrowing: regulators are progressively integrating climate risks into prudential requirements, and ESG standards are becoming conditions precedent in institutional financing.\n\n2.3 Sustainable finance vs ESG\n\nSustainable finance refers to the integration of ESG criteria into financing and investment decisions (green bonds, sustainability-linked loans, climate finance). ESG is the measurement framework; sustainable finance is the operational application of that framework.\n\n2.4 Non-financial reporting vs prudential reporting\n\nNon-financial reporting (GRI, ISSB) covers the institution's environmental, social and governance impacts. Prudential reporting (BCEAO, COBAC) covers solvency, liquidity, portfolio quality and operational risks. Both are complementary but distinct.",

      "3. Regional ESG framework: BCEAO and COBAC\n\n3.1 BCEAO — UEMOA zone\n\nThe Central Bank of West African States (BCEAO) develops prudential regulation applicable to financial institutions in the UEMOA zone. To date, there is no fully codified ESG prudential framework comparable to European regulations (CSRD, SFDR, Green Taxonomy).\n\nHowever, the BCEAO is progressively strengthening the integration of climate, environmental and social risks in its supervisory approaches:\n- Integration of climate risks into the Risk-Based Approach (RBA)\n- Prudential instructions governing exposure to sensitive sectors (agriculture, mining, energy)\n- Enhanced reporting requirements on non-performing loans linked to environmental factors\n\nSource: BCEAO, Prudential Instructions — bceao.int\n\n3.2 COBAC — CEMAC zone\n\nThe Banking Commission of Central Africa (COBAC) is the prudential supervisory authority of the CEMAC zone. Like the BCEAO, COBAC has not published a complete codified ESG framework to date.\n\nCOBAC prudential orientations nevertheless integrate:\n- Evaluation of climate risk exposure in credit portfolios\n- Monitoring of sectoral receivables sensitive to environmental shocks\n- Consideration of physical risks (floods, droughts) in provision analysis\n\nSource: COBAC, Prudential Orientations — cobac.org\n\n3.3 Current regulatory stance\n\nNeither the BCEAO nor COBAC have imposed mandatory ESG reporting for all financial institutions to date. Current ESG obligations in French-speaking Africa primarily stem from:\n- Multilateral financing agreements (IFC, BOAD, AfDB, AFD)\n- Voluntary institutional commitments (PRI, SBTi membership, etc.)\n- Specific sectoral requirements (infrastructure projects subject to Equator Principles, agricultural projects subject to IFC EHS Guidelines)\n\nThis situation may evolve as international standards (ISSB, TCFD) gain regional adoption.",

      "4. The 3 ESG pillars and their application to financial institutions in Africa\n\n4.1 Environment (E)\n\n- Exposed sectors: agriculture (drought risk, deforestation), energy (energy transition), mining (biodiversity, water), infrastructure\n- Compliance with national Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA) and IFC EHS Guidelines\n- Waste management, water and energy consumption in institutional operations\n- Climate risk exposure assessment in the credit portfolio\n\nIndicative data: Climate-related event losses in Sub-Saharan Africa represent a significant share of GDP according to the World Bank's Climate Change Knowledge Portal, with considerable variability by country and sector. Any ESG data must be sourced, dated and contextualized.\n\nSource: World Bank, Climate Change Knowledge Portal — worldbank.org\n\n4.2 Social (S)\n\n- Financial inclusion: access of vulnerable populations to financial services, with traceable and verifiable indicators\n- Fight against child labor and forced labor in financed value chains (ILO Conventions C138, C182)\n- Gender equity in governance and workforce\n- Client data protection and AML/CFT compliance\n- Health and safety of communities impacted by financed projects (IFC PS 4)\n\n4.3 Governance (G)\n\n- Board composition and independence (OHADA AUSCGIE revised 2014; OECD governance principles)\n- Effective separation of powers between Board Chairman and CEO\n- Anti-corruption and AML/CFT compliance\n- Financial transparency and SYSCOHADA/AUDCIF-compliant regulatory reporting\n- Executive remuneration aligned with long-term performance and ESG criteria\n- Organizational ethics and formalized code of conduct",

      "5. Institutional ESG governance\n\n5.1 ESG Committee at Board level\n\nEstablishing an ESG Committee (or Sustainability Committee) at Board level is a recommended practice per OECD standards and IFC guidelines. Its responsibilities include:\n- Oversight of ESG policy and implementation\n- Validation of ESG indicators and alert thresholds\n- Review of sustainability reports before publication\n- Monitoring of climate and social risk exposure\n- Review of external commitments (PRI, SBTi, Equator Principles)\n\nRecommended composition: at least 3 Board members including one independent director with ESG or risk expertise.\n\nSource: OECD, Corporate Governance Principles — oecd.org\n\n5.2 Three Lines of Defense applied to ESG\n\nFirst line (operational):\n- Business teams integrate ESG criteria into decision-making processes\n- Credit analysis grids include sectoral ESG criteria\n\nSecond line (control and compliance):\n- Compliance function ensures alignment of practices with ESG policy\n- Permanent control evaluates ESG data collection and reliability\n\nThird line (internal audit):\n- Internal audit evaluates ESG framework effectiveness, data reliability and standards compliance\n- Annual audit plan covers critical ESG processes\n\nSource: Institute of Internal Auditors (IIA), Three Lines Model — theiia.org\n\n5.3 ESG Materiality\n\nESG materiality identifies environmental, social and governance issues that have a significant impact on strategy, financial performance and value creation. GRI adopts a double materiality approach:\n- Outward impact: institution's impact on ESG issues\n- Inward impact: ESG issues' impact on the institution (financial materiality)\n\nMateriality determination process:\n1. Stakeholder mapping (clients, investors, regulators, communities, employees)\n2. ESG issues identification by sector (SASB sector standards, GRI sector standards)\n3. Impact assessment per dimension\n4. Prioritization and selection of material topics to disclose\n\nSource: GRI 3: Material Topics 2021 — globalreporting.org\n\n5.4 ESG Data Governance\n\nESG data reliability is a prerequisite for any reporting and audit process:\n- Traceability: each ESG indicator must be sourced, dated and attributed to a business owner\n- Verifiability: data must be auditable by an independent third party\n- Consistency: stable methodology applied across periods\n- Coverage: full operational perimeter covered (no geographic or sectoral gaps)\n- Compliance with data protection standards (ISO 27001, national data protection legislation)\n\nSource: IFAC, ESG Data and Reporting — ifac.org",

      "6. ESG audit and internal control\n\n6.1 External ESG audit\n\nExternal ESG audit is an independent assurance engagement covering:\n- Compliance of the sustainability report with retained standards (GRI, ISSB, SASB)\n- Existence and effectiveness of internal ESG processes\n- Reliability of declared ESG indicators\n- Absence of greenwashing in communications and reporting\n\nExternal ESG audit is required by major international financiers (IFC, BOAD, AfDB) for sustainability reports submitted under financing agreements.\n\nSource: IFAC, International Framework for Assurance Engagements — ifac.org\n\n6.2 Internal ESG control\n\n- ESG risk mapping (climate, social, governance, reputational)\n- Control plan associated with identified risks\n- Control performance indicators (coverage rates, remediation timelines)\n- Alert escalation on ESG deviations and incidents\n- Regular reporting to ESG Committee and Board\n\n6.3 Anti-greenwashing\n\nGreenwashing consists of presenting a favorable ESG image not corroborated by verifiable data. Prevention measures:\n- Ban on absolute unverifiable claims ('100% sustainable', 'zero impact')\n- Systematic sourcing of each ESG indicator\n- Third-party validation before publication\n- Alignment of marketing communications with audited sustainability report",

      "7. Climate risks and stress testing\n\n7.1 Physical risks\n\n- Acute risks: floods, cyclones, droughts, heatwaves affecting agricultural receivables and financed infrastructure\n- Chronic risks: progressive temperature rise, sea level rise, soil degradation reducing agricultural borrowers' productivity\n\n7.2 Transition risks\n\n- Policy and regulatory risks: new carbon taxes, sectoral bans, environmental standards\n- Technological risks: obsolescence of financed carbon-intensive technologies\n- Market risks: evolving consumer and investor preferences\n- Reputational risks: institution's association with carbon-intensive projects\n\n7.3 Climate stress testing\n\nClimate stress testing evaluates the portfolio's resilience against extreme climate scenarios, aligned with TCFD recommendations and IFRS S2 requirements.\n\nMethodology:\n1. Climate scenario selection (NGFS: orderly, disorderly, 'hot house world')\n2. Modeling of impact on sectoral receivables (agriculture, real estate, energy)\n3. Assessment of impact on provisions, regulatory capital and liquidity\n4. Board reporting and integration into risk appetite\n\nSource: NGFS, Climate Scenarios for Central Banks and Supervisors — ngfs.net\nSource: World Bank, Climate Change Knowledge Portal — worldbank.org",

      "8. Green taxonomy and sustainable finance\n\n8.1 Green taxonomy\n\nGreen taxonomy classifies economic activities considered sustainable according to objective criteria (substantial contribution to environmental objectives, Do No Significant Harm — DNSH, social safeguards).\n\nNo official green taxonomy has been adopted at the UEMOA or CEMAC level to date. Institutions may align with existing taxonomies:\n- EU Green Taxonomy (Regulation EU 2020/852)\n- Climate Bonds Initiative (CBI) Taxonomy\n- IFC sectoral standards and EHS Guidelines\n\n8.2 Sustainable finance instruments\n\n- Green bonds: bonds whose proceeds are allocated to environmental projects\n- Sustainability-linked loans (SLL): loans with rates tied to ESG target achievement\n- Social bonds: bonds financing social projects (inclusion, education, health)\n- Sustainability bonds: combining environmental and social components\n\nSource: Climate Bonds Initiative — climatebonds.net\nSource: Loan Market Association, Sustainability-Linked Loan Principles — loanmarketassoc.com",

      "9. ESG non-compliance risks\n\n1. Loss of access to international financing: multilateral lenders (IFC, BOAD, AfDB, AFD, EIB) include ESG clauses in financing agreements. Absence of compliant sustainability reporting can lead to financing rejection or suspension.\n\n2. Potential regulatory sanctions: if BCEAO or COBAC strengthen prudential requirements on climate risks, unprepared institutions may face corrective measures or disciplinary sanctions.\n\n3. Loss of institutional clients: large corporations, impact funds and international NGOs select financial partners based on documented ESG criteria.\n\n4. Reputational risk: greenwashing allegations or social standards violations are amplified by international media and can trigger deposit withdrawals or exclusion from financing networks.\n\n5. Direct financial risk: unmanaged exposure to physical and transition climate risks can result in credit losses, increased provisions and degraded prudential ratios.\n\nSource: World Bank, Environmental and Social Framework — worldbank.org\nSource: IFC, Sustainability Framework — ifc.org",

      "OPERATIONAL CHECKLIST — ESG Framework Structuring\n\nPhase A — ESG Diagnosis (J0 to J45)\n- ESG maturity assessment per multi-reference grid (GRI, IFC, ISSB, SASB)\n- Environmental risk mapping of portfolio (exposed sectors: agriculture, energy, mining, infrastructure)\n- Social risk mapping (financial inclusion, gender, working conditions in value chains)\n- Governance risk mapping (board independence, AML/CFT, transparency)\n- Gap analysis vs. identified donors' requirements\n- ESG benchmark vs. comparable UEMOA/CEMAC institutions\n- ESG materiality determination (double materiality GRI 3 approach)\n\nPhase B — Framework structuring (J45 to J120)\n- Drafting of ESG policy integrated into credit, investment and risk policies\n- ESG Committee establishment within the Board\n- ESG KPI definition with calculation methodology, sources and responsible owners\n- ESG criteria integration into credit and investment analysis grids\n- ESG data governance implementation (traceability, verifiability, consistency)\n- ESG and climate risk training for business teams, management and Board\n- Climate stress testing plan development (NGFS scenarios, exposed sectors)\n\nPhase C — Reporting and audit (J120 to J240)\n- First sustainability report production compliant with retained standards (GRI, ISSB or IFC)\n- Internal ESG audit of framework and report\n- External audit preparation (if required by donors)\n- Report submission to donors and institutional investors\n- Publication on institution's website\n- Annual review, indicator update and continuous improvement\n\nFinal deliverable: sustainability report compliant with international standards, operational ESG framework integrated into credit and governance processes.",

      "CRITICAL RISKS — The 7 errors that undermine ESG credibility\n\n1. ESG report without verifiable data: A sustainability report without quantified indicators, source traceability and external audit has no institutional value.\n\n2. ESG/CSR confusion: Presenting philanthropic actions as ESG compliance evidence is a major conceptual error. ESG is a governance and reporting framework, not a social marketing tool.\n\n3. Absence of operational integration: An ESG policy not embedded in credit grids, ALM procedures and decision processes remains a theoretical document.\n\n4. Unsourced ESG data: Any declared data must be sourced, dated and verifiable. Unsourced data exposes the institution to greenwashing accusations.\n\n5. Climate risk underestimation: Failure to assess climate risk exposure in agricultural, real estate and energy portfolios is a major governance gap per TCFD recommendations and IFRS S2 requirements.\n\n6. Absence of ESG materiality: Producing an exhaustive report on all ESG issues without materiality prioritization dilutes the message and masks genuinely material risks.\n\n7. Insufficient team training: Business teams, management and Board must understand ESG criteria to apply them. Systematic training is a prerequisite for durable compliance.",

      "METHODOLOGY — KHEPRA ESG\u2122: methodological accompaniment framework\n\nKHEPRA ESG\u2122 is Khepra Experts' methodological framework for accompanying financial institutions in structuring their ESG framework in Francophone Africa. It does not constitute a guarantee of regulatory compliance or financing, but a structured diagnostic, structuring and reporting device.\n\nPillar 1 — Diagnosis and materiality: assess ESG maturity, map sectoral ESG risks and determine materiality per double materiality approach. Deliverables: structured ESG maturity score, risk mapping, gap analysis vs. targeted standards.\n\nPillar 2 — Governance and operational integration: structure ESG at Board level (ESG Committee), business processes (ESG credit grid) and internal control (Three Lines of Defense). Deliverables: integrated ESG policy, committee established, ESG KPIs defined and sourced, training plan.\n\nPillar 3 — Reporting and assurance: produce sustainability report compliant with retained standards, prepare external audit and structure ESG communications. Deliverables: sustainability report, audit documentation, continuous improvement plan.\n\nIndicative timeline: 240 days for complete ESG framework structuring, subject to team mobilization and available data quality.",

      "ILLUSTRATIVE FICTIONAL CASE — ESG structuring of a regional bank (fictional data for educational purposes)\n\nFictional context: A regional bank in the UEMOA zone seeks access to multilateral financing. Condition precedent: production of a sustainability report compliant with international standards.\n\nInitial KHEPRA ESG\u2122 diagnosis (fictional data):\n- ESG maturity score: 38/100 (indicatively insufficient)\n- Absence of formal integrated ESG policy\n- No ESG indicators in monthly reporting\n- Climate risk not assessed in agricultural portfolio (35% of portfolio, indicative)\n- Women in management positions: 12% (fictional data, no reference to uncoded IFC target)\n\nStructuring actions (methodological illustration):\n- Drafting of ESG policy integrated into credit policies\n- ESG Committee formation with independent directors\n- 12 monthly ESG KPIs defined with calculation methodology and sources\n- ESG criteria integration into agricultural credit analysis grids\n- ESG training plan for business teams and Board\n- First sustainability report produced per GRI/IFC standards per targeted donors\n\nIllustrative result: Final ESG score: 82/100 (fictional). This illustrative case does not prejudice any financing disbursement, which rests solely on lenders' assessment.",

      "CONSOLIDATED ESG BIBLIOGRAPHY\n\n| Reference | Organization | Document | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| IFC Performance Standards | IFC | Performance Standards on Environmental and Social Sustainability | 2012 |\n| IFC Sustainability Framework | IFC | Sustainability Framework | 2012 |\n| IFC EHS Guidelines | IFC | Environmental, Health and Safety Guidelines | 2007-2024 |\n| GRI Universal Standards | GRI | GRI 1, 2, 3 Universal Standards | 2021 |\n| GRI Sector Standards | GRI | Sector Standards (Financial Services, Agriculture, etc.) | 2021-2024 |\n| PRI Principles | UN PRI | Principles for Responsible Investment | 2006 |\n| IFRS S1 | ISSB / IFRS Foundation | Sustainability-related Disclosures | 2023 |\n| IFRS S2 | ISSB / IFRS Foundation | Climate-related Disclosures | 2023 |\n| TCFD Recommendations | FSB | Recommendations of the TCFD | 2017 |\n| SASB Standards | IFRS Foundation / SASB | Sustainability Accounting Standards | 2018-2022 |\n| TNFD Framework | TNFD | Nature-related Financial Disclosures Framework | 2023 |\n| Equator Principles EP4 | EP Association | Equator Principles IV | 2020 |\n| ISO 14001 | ISO | Environmental Management Systems | 2015 |\n| ISO 26000 | ISO | Social Responsibility Guidelines | 2010 |\n| ISO 27001 | ISO | Information Security Management Systems | 2022 |\n| SBTi Criteria | SBTi | Science Based Targets Criteria | 2021 |\n| NGFS Scenarios | NGFS | Climate Scenarios for Central Banks | 2023 |\n| OECD Principles | OECD | Corporate Governance Principles | 2015/revisions |\n| Climate Bonds Taxonomy | CBI | Green Bond Taxonomy | 2023 |\n| LMA SLL Principles | LMA | Sustainability-Linked Loan Principles | 2023 |\n| World Bank ESF | World Bank | Environmental and Social Framework | 2016 |\n| BCEAO Instructions | BCEAO | Prudential instructions SFD and banks | In force |\n| COBAC Orientations | COBAC | Prudential orientations | In force |",

      "REGULATORY ESG FAQ\n\nQ: What is ESG compliance for a financial institution in Francophone Africa?\nR: ESG compliance refers to the set of practices, processes and reporting enabling a financial institution to demonstrate structured management of its environmental, social and governance risks and impacts. It is primarily conditioned by multilateral development finance institution requirements (IFC, BOAD, AfDB, AFD) and voluntary commitments (PRI, SBTi). Neither BCEAO nor COBAC have imposed mandatory universal ESG reporting to date, though they are progressively strengthening climate risk integration in prudential supervision.\n\nQ: What ESG standards apply in the UEMOA and CEMAC zones?\nR: Reference standards include: IFC Performance Standards (8 standards), GRI Standards (Universal Standards 2021 and sectoral), UN PRI, ISSB standards (IFRS S1 and S2), TCFD recommendations (integrated in IFRS S2), SASB (sectoral financial materiality), TNFD (nature risks), Equator Principles (infrastructure/industrial projects), ISO 14001/26000/27001, and SBTi/Net Zero frameworks. BCEAO and COBAC integrate climate risks without a standalone codified ESG framework comparable to European standards.\n\nQ: Do BCEAO or COBAC impose mandatory ESG reporting?\nR: No. As of this publication date, neither BCEAO nor COBAC have published a fully codified ESG prudential framework imposing mandatory ESG reporting on all financial institutions. Current ESG obligations stem from multilateral financing agreements, voluntary commitments and specific sectoral requirements. This situation may evolve.\n\nQ: What is the difference between ESG and CSR?\nR: ESG is an extra-financial evaluation framework structured around quantified metrics, standardized reporting and integration into investment decisions. CSR is a voluntary ethical, social and environmental engagement approach that may include philanthropic actions without necessarily involving institutional reporting. ESG is not CSR.\n\nQ: How long does it take to structure an ESG framework?\nR: The indicative timeline for complete ESG framework structuring (diagnosis, governance, operational integration, reporting and audit) is 240 days, subject to team mobilization, available data quality and targeted standards.\n\nQ: Is external ESG audit mandatory?\nR: External ESG audit is not a universal regulatory obligation imposed by BCEAO or COBAC. It is however required by major multilateral lenders (IFC, BOAD, AfDB) for sustainability reports submitted under financing agreements.\n\nQ: What is greenwashing and how to avoid it?\nR: Greenwashing is presenting a favorable ESG image not corroborated by verifiable data. Prevention: source every ESG indicator, avoid absolute unverifiable claims, align marketing communications with the audited sustainability report, submit reporting to external audit before publication.\n\nQ: What is double materiality?\nR: Double materiality (GRI 3) considers an ESG issue material if it has a significant impact on the institution's strategy and financial performance (financial materiality), AND if the institution has a significant impact on that issue (impact materiality). This approach differs from financial materiality alone (SASB).",

      "CTA CONVERSION: Assess your institution's ESG maturity with the KHEPRA ESG\u2122 methodological framework. Request a structured diagnosis. Our team will analyze your current framework, identify gaps versus international standards and deliver a prioritized action plan within 5 business days.",

      "DISCLAIMER AND LEGAL NOTICE\n\nThis reference document is provided for informational and educational purposes only. It does not constitute legal, tax, accounting, prudential or investment advice. Information is based on regulatory texts, standards and official publications in force as of the drafting date. Banking, financial and ESG standards evolve regularly; it is the responsibility of directors, advisors and stakeholders to verify applicable law and standards at the time of their actions.\n\nKhepra Experts disclaims all liability for the use of information contained herein. The KHEPRA ESG\u2122 methodological framework is a structured accompaniment device; it does not constitute a guarantee of regulatory compliance, certification or financing disbursement. Any ESG structuring, file submission or lender submission decision must be taken in consultation with qualified legal, accounting and prudential advisors."
